<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/21st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Intifada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2000">2000</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Al-Aqsa Intifada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Intifada was the far bloodier Palestinian uprising that erupted in September 2000 after the collapse of final-status peace talks. Suicide bombings in Israeli cities and large Israeli military operations in Palestinian towns killed around a thousand Israelis and three times as many Palestinians, buried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> peace process, and led Israel to build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> barrier and withdraw its settlers from Gaza in 2005.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo-accords">Oslo Accords</a>, signed in 1993 and 1995, Israel committed to a phased withdrawal of its forces from parts of the Gaza Strip and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> and affirmed Palestinian self-government there through a Palestinian Authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">Palestine Liberation Organization</a> formally recognised Israel and took on responsibility for internal security in the evacuated areas. Self-rule was to last five years while a permanent agreement was negotiated, but both sides were deeply disappointed with the process and blamed each other for its failure.</p><p>From 11 to 25 July 2000 the Camp David summit brought together US President Bill Clinton, Israeli Prime Minister Ehud Barak and Palestinian Authority Chairman Yasser Arafat. The talks failed, each side blaming the other.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 28 September 2000 the Israeli opposition leader Ariel Sharon visited the Temple Mount, also known as the Haram al-Sharif, with a Likud delegation guarded by hundreds of riot police. He said that it was the holiest site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and the right of every Jew to visit it. Palestinians saw the visit as provocative, and protests and riots followed, which Israeli forces dispersed with tear gas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> bullets and live ammunition. In the first five days Israeli forces killed 47 Palestinians, and Palestinians killed five Israelis.</p><p>Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intifada">First Intifada</a> of 1987 to 1993, which was a civil uprising of mass protests and strikes, the Second Intifada rapidly became an armed conflict. Palestinian groups including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hamas</a>, Palestinian Islamic Jihad, the Popular Front for the Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and the al-Aqsa Martyrs' Brigades carried out shootings, rocket attacks and about 138 suicide bombings after March 2001, mostly aimed at Israeli civilians. The Israel Defense Forces responded with incursions into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> and Gaza Strip, airstrikes and targeted killings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The violence is estimated to have killed about 3,000 Palestinians, 1,000 Israelis and 64 foreign nationals. The uprising ended with the Sharm el-Sheikh summit of 2005, where the Palestinian president Mahmoud Abbas and Prime Minister Sharon agreed to de-escalate and reaffirmed the roadmap for peace proposed by the Quartet in 2003. Israel's disengagement from the Gaza Strip was completed in August 2005. The ending date is disputed, since no single event closed it; some place it in late 2004, after Arafat's death. Israeli public support for the Oslo Accords fell, according to surveys, from 39% in May 2000 to 26% in May 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Intifada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
